#!/usr/bin/env -S python3 -u
"""
arcs-page: render `work-arcs --json` as the standing picture of your work.

The first version of this page listed artifacts -- 97 stash rows, 261 branch rows --
and was correctly called useless: `stash@{38}` on its own says nothing, and a flat
list of branches is the same wall of text in a nicer font. The unit was wrong.

This renders arcs, and each card leads with what the arc IS rather than with how much
of it there is. A count is an input to a judgement, not the judgement: opening a card
with "112 unpushed / 15 branches / 19 sessions / 7 stacked" is what made the previous
version a spreadsheet in a nicer font, so the counts moved to the bottom of the card
and the description arc-brief derived moved to the top.

Under each description is the arc's spawn tree. work-arcs has always derived which
branch each branch was cut from; the previous version printed a flat list of the four
with the most commits, so the structure was computed on every run and discarded at the
last step.

  work-arcs --json | arc-brief | arcs-page --out page.html

Renders without arc-brief in the pipeline too -- the cards fall back to the cluster
label and lose their descriptions, which is the honest degradation.
"""

import argparse
import html
import json
import sys
import urllib.parse
from datetime import datetime
from pathlib import Path

